<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madeira-Mamoré Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Madeira-Mamoré Railway was a line of about 366 km built from 1907 to 1912 through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon rainforest</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rondonia">Rondônia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, to bypass the rapids of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a> and Mamoré rivers. Brazil promised it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-petropolis">Treaty of Petrópolis</a> of 1903 to give Bolivian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> an outlet to the Atlantic. Thousands of workers died of disease during its construction, and the line closed in 1972.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madeira-mamore-railway.jpg" alt="Madeira-Mamoré Railway" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Svetlov Artem · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madeira-Mamor%C3%A9_Railroad_map_2025_openstreetmap.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
